INT. ICE RINK - RESURFACER GARAGE - NIGHT

A concrete bay with a hulking resurfacing machine, drips from the ceiling, a flickering overhead tube. The big door is shut. The cold bites.

ROXANNE (30s, parka) crouches by the machine's wheel with a flashlight. GUS (50s, rink manager, keys on a lanyard) holds the clipboard. TAMSIN (teen, hockey jersey, skates over her shoulder) stays near the door.

GUS
Roxanne, the water tank's been leaking since Tuesday.

ROXANNE
It's not water, Gus.

She points the flashlight. A dark red trail runs from the machine's blade toward the drain.

TAMSIN
I'm going to get my dad.

GUS
Tamsin, stay here.

TAMSIN
It smells like pennies, Mr. Gus.

The machine's engine coughs once. Nobody touched it. Gus drops his clipboard. Roxanne stands.

Under the machine's belly, a FIGURE uncurls, grey and frost-furred, long fingers dragging the concrete.

ROXANNE
Don't move.

The Figure rises. Its blue lips shape words that make no sound. It takes a step toward Tamsin.

Gus swings his keys like a flail. The Figure catches his wrist. They struggle. Roxanne grabs a pry bar and slams it into the thing's ribs. It shrieks, soundless, and throws them both against the wall.

TAMSIN
Run!

All three scatter. The Figure watches them go, then folds back under the machine, patient.
